solution:
Note that $\mathcal{P}$ can be seen to be the union of rectangles aligned to the same axis. Let $w = j - k$. We claim the answer is then equal to
\[
	4 \cdot \min (k - w, 1).
\]
If $k - w \le 0$, we may cut a rectangle into $k+w$ rectangles and shade non-adjacent ones. If $k - w > 0$, we cut a rectangle into $2w+1$ rectangles and shade every other piece such that we shade the ending. We then cut one of the ending pieces into $k - (w+1) + 1 = k - w$ distinct shaded rectangles, which creates an additional $4 \cdot ((k-w)-1)$ corners as desired.

Here is an example for $w = 2, k = 4$:

[asy]
path rect = (0,0)--(0,3)--(1,3)--(1,0)--cycle;
pen shade = lightgrey;
filldraw(rect, shade);
filldraw(shift(1,0)*rect, white);
filldraw(shift(2,0)*rect, shade);
filldraw(shift(3,0)*rect, white);
filldraw(shift(4,0)*((0,0)--(0,1)--(1,1)--(1,0)--cycle), shade);
filldraw(shift(4,0)*((0,2)--(0,3)--(1,3)--(1,2)--cycle), shade);
[/asy]

As for bounding, we claim the following:

Claim: There exists an injection between the shaded corners in the partition that aren't corners of $\mathcal{P}$ and white corners.
Proof: We construct this injection for the set of shaded corners iteratively. Consider any such black corner. If another shaded corner has the same corner, then it must follow that there exists two unshaded corners bordering these corners, so pair these arbitrarily. Else, it follows that either this shaded corner is on the side or in the interior of $\mathcal{P}$. We may check that this forces a corresponding white corner in either case. $\blacksquare$

This equality case demonstrates how it works for a shaded corner on the inside or interior, and a checkerboard coloring demonstrates how it works for two shaded corners bordering.

[asy]
path rect = (0,0)--(0,3)--(1,3)--(1,0)--cycle;
pen shade = lightgrey;
filldraw((0,0)--(0,3)--(1,3)--(1,0)--cycle, shade);
filldraw((0,4)--(3,4)--(3,5)--(0,5)--cycle, shade);
filldraw(shift(4,2)*((0,0)--(0,3)--(1,3)--(1,0)--cycle), shade);
filldraw(shift(2,-4)*((0,4)--(3,4)--(3,5)--(0,5)--cycle), shade);

filldraw(shift(1,0)*((0,0)--(0,3)--(1,3)--(1,0)--cycle), white);
filldraw((shift(0,-1)*((0,4)--(3,4)--(3,5)--(0,5)--cycle)), white);
filldraw(shift(3,2)*((0,0)--(0,3)--(1,3)--(1,0)--cycle), white);
filldraw(shift(2,-3)*((0,4)--(3,4)--(3,5)--(0,5)--cycle), white);

filldraw(shift(2,2)*unitsquare, shade);
[/asy]


This means that there are at least $4 \cdot (k-w)$ edges. Since $\mathcal{P}$ only has right angle corners, it is easy to check it can't have three sides.

false_statement:
A piecewise linear function is defined by
\[f(x) = \begin{cases} x & \text{if } x \in [-1, 1) \\ 2 - x & \text{if } x \in [1, 3)\end{cases}\]
and $f(x + 4) = f(x)$ for all real numbers $x.$ The graph of $f(x)$ has the sawtooth pattern depicted below. 

\begin{asy}
import graph;

size(18cm);
real f(real x) {
    real x_mod = x - 4 * floor((x+1) / 4);
    if (-1 <= x_mod && x_mod < 1) {
        return x_mod;
    } else if (1 <= x_mod && x_mod < 3) {
        return 2 - x_mod;
    }
    return 0; // Should never reach here
}

// Set up axes
real xmin = -8, xmax = 11, ymin = -2, ymax = 2;
xaxis("", xmin, -2, Ticks(2.0));
xaxis("", -2, 2);
xaxis("", 2, xmax, Ticks(2.0));
yaxis("", ymin, ymax, Ticks(2.0));
// Plot the function in segments due to periodicity
real step = 0.01;
for (real k = -8; k < 7; k += 4) {
    draw(graph(f, k, k + 4, n=(int)((4)/step)),blue);
}
    draw(graph(f, 8, 11, n=(int)((4)/step)),blue);
\end{asy}

The parabola $x = 34y^2$ intersects the graph of $f(x)$ at finitely many points. Show that the sum of the $y$-coordinates of these intersection points can be expressed is $\tfrac{3 + 5\sqrt{185}}{68}.$

